  var PLANET_INTERIOR={mercury:{layers:[{name:'Solid inner core',pct:25,color:'#a0a0a0',label:'Iron (Fe)',desc:'Pure iron crystallized under ~40 GPa pressure. ~1,000 km radius. Temp ~2,600\u00b0F. Detected via MESSENGER libration and geodetic tracking (Genova et al. 2019, GRL).'},{name:'Liquid outer core',pct:52,color:'#c8a060',label:'Iron-nickel-sulfur (Fe-Ni-S)',desc:'Molten Fe-Ni-Si with ~5-15% sulfur. Convection here generates Mercury\'s weak magnetic field (~1% of Earth\'s). Total core fills ~83% of planet radius \u2014 the largest core-to-planet ratio in the solar system.'},{name:'FeS stratified layer',pct:6,color:'#e0c080',label:'Molten iron sulfide (FeS)',desc:'A buoyant iron-sulfide "snow" layer at the top of the outer core, predicted from MESSENGER magnetic data and thermal models. Depleted of Si, enriched in S. Will be tested by ESA/JAXA\'s BepiColombo, arriving in orbit late 2026.'},{name:'Mantle',pct:15.5,color:'#8a6040',label:'Silicate rock (olivine + pyroxene)',desc:'Thin silicate mantle only ~400 km thick. Magnesium-rich silicates (olivine, pyroxene). Unusually thin \u2014 a giant impact may have stripped most of it ~4.5 Bya.'},{name:'Crust',pct:1.5,color:'#706050',label:'Basalt (volcanic rock)',desc:'~25-50 km basaltic crust (~1.5% of radius). Heavily cratered. Volcanic smooth plains from ancient lava floods. Contains graphite \u2014 the planet may have once had a carbon-rich primordial crust.'}],note:'Mercury has the largest core-to-planet ratio in the solar system (~83% of radius, ~60% of volume). MESSENGER magnetic data and Genova et al. 2019 libration analysis confirmed a solid inner core. BepiColombo (orbit insertion late 2026) will refine the inner/outer core boundary and test the predicted FeS stratified layer.'},venus:{layers:[{name:'Core',pct:53,color:'#c89860',label:'Liquid iron-nickel (Fe-Ni)',desc:'Iron-nickel core ~3,200 km radius \u2014 53% of Venus\'s radius. Margot et al. 2021 (Nature Astronomy) made the first direct measurement via Earth-based radar but could not resolve solid vs liquid. Most post-2023 reviews (O\'Rourke et al.) tentatively favor a fully liquid or mostly liquid core \u2014 no confirmed inner core. The absence of vigorous convection explains Venus\'s complete lack of a magnetic field.'},{name:'Mantle',pct:46,color:'#a07050',label:'Silicate rock (peridotite)',desc:'Thick silicate mantle ~2,800 km. Peridotite (olivine + pyroxene). Mantle convection drives periodic catastrophic resurfacing every ~500 Myr where most of the crust melts and reforms.'},{name:'Crust',pct:1,color:'#c8a878',label:'Basalt (volcanic rock)',desc:'~20-50 km basaltic crust (~0.5-0.8% of radius). No tectonic plates \u2014 a single stagnant lid punctuated by massive volcanic events. Surface is ~500 Myr old, the youngest of any rocky planet.'}],note:'Margot et al. 2021 (Nature Astronomy) gave the first direct measurement of Venus\'s core via Earth-based radar. Core size is known (~53% of radius) but the solid/liquid state is unresolved \u2014 current consensus leans liquid. Unlike Earth, Venus has no magnetic field, no plate tectonics, and no water. Upcoming missions: VERITAS, DAVINCI+, EnVision (early 2030s).'},mars:{layers:[{name:'Solid inner core',pct:17,color:'#b07838',label:'Iron-nickel (Fe-Ni)',desc:'Solid Fe-Ni inner core ~613 km radius (Nature, Dec 2025). Detected via PKKP and PKiKP seismic phases \u2014 the first detection of an inner core on a planet besides Earth. Overturned earlier InSight results that suggested an entirely liquid core.'},{name:'Liquid outer core',pct:23,color:'#c89050',label:'Iron-sulfur-oxygen (Fe-S-O)',desc:'Molten Fe alloy outer core, rich in sulfur (~15-17%) and oxygen. Total core ~1,830 km radius = 54% of planet radius. Light elements make it far less dense than Earth\u2019s core. The small solid inner core cannot sustain a dynamo \u2014 explains Mars losing its magnetic field ~4 Bya.'},{name:'Molten silicate basal layer',pct:5,color:'#d04820',label:'Molten silicate rock',desc:'~150-180 km partially molten silicate layer at the core-mantle boundary. Khan et al. 2023 and Samuel et al. 2023 (Nature) reinterpreted the S1222a marsquake and showed this layer explains the anomalously low-density signal earlier attributed to the core itself. Unique to Mars, thermally insulates the core.'},{name:'Mantle',pct:53.2,color:'#a06838',label:'Silicate rock (olivine + pyroxene)',desc:'Single silicate mantle (olivine + pyroxene). Mars is too small for the bridgmanite phase transition Earth has at its lower mantle \u2014 pressure at the CMB is only ~21 GPa. More iron-rich than Earth\'s mantle (gives Mars its red color). Lithosphere is ~500 km thick (vs Earth\'s ~100 km), so mantle convection has largely ceased.'},{name:'Crust',pct:1.8,color:'#c04020',label:'Basalt + iron oxide (Fe\u2082O\u2083)',desc:'Dichotomy: northern lowlands ~30 km thin, southern highlands ~60 km thick. Basaltic composition coated in iron oxide dust (rust \u2192 red color). Contains hydrated minerals proving ancient water.'}],note:'InSight (2018-2022) detected 1,319 marsquakes. Key results: (1) Khan 2023 / Samuel 2023 identified a molten silicate layer at the core-mantle boundary. (2) A Dec 2025 Nature paper detected a small solid inner core via PKKP phases \u2014 the first inner core detection on a planet besides Earth. (3) Mars has no bridgmanite lower mantle because the planet is too small for the required pressure.'},jupiter:{layers:[{name:'Dilute core',pct:55,color:'#a89070',label:'Heavy-element mixture (rock, metals, H\u2082O)',desc:'Juno gravity (Wahl 2017, Militzer 2022, Miguel 2022) revealed NOT a compact core but a dilute/fuzzy core extending to ~50-63% of Jupiter\'s radius. Heavy elements (rock, ice, metals \u2014 ~10-20 Earth masses) are gradationally dissolved into surrounding hydrogen. No sharp inner boundary. Temp ~35,000\u00b0F, pressure ~100 million atmospheres at center.'},{name:'Metallic hydrogen',pct:28,color:'#6878a8',label:'Metallic hydrogen (H\u2082)',desc:'At >2 million atmospheres, hydrogen becomes a liquid metal \u2014 conducts electricity like copper. Grades outward from the dilute core to ~85% of radius. Generates Jupiter\'s magnetic field (20,000\u00d7 Earth\'s). No spacecraft has survived this deep.'},{name:'Liquid molecular hydrogen',pct:14,color:'#88a0c0',label:'Liquid hydrogen/helium (H\u2082/He)',desc:'Liquid hydrogen and helium envelope. Gradually transitions from metallic hydrogen below to gaseous atmosphere above \u2014 no sharp boundary. Helium "rain" may precipitate downward through this layer.'},{name:'Atmosphere',pct:3,color:'#d8b880',label:'Hydrogen + helium (H\u2082 89% + He 10%)',desc:'Visible cloud decks: ammonia ice (top, white), ammonium hydrosulfide (middle, brown), water ice (lowest, hidden). Zone/belt structure from 400 mph jet streams. Great Red Spot extends ~300 km deep.'}],note:'Juno (orbiting since 2016) fundamentally rewrote Jupiter\'s interior. The compact core of pre-2017 textbooks is gone \u2014 replaced by a fuzzy/dilute core extending to ~60% of the radius with heavy elements gradationally mixed into the metallic-hydrogen envelope. Jupiter radiates 1.6\u00d7 more energy than it receives from the Sun (Kelvin-Helmholtz contraction).'},saturn:{layers:[{name:'Diffuse core',pct:60,color:'#a89070',label:'Rock, ice, hydrogen (H)',desc:'Ring-seismology (Mankovich & Fuller 2021, Nature Astronomy) detected f-mode oscillations in Saturn\'s C ring that revealed a fuzzy/diffuse core extending to ~60% of Saturn\'s radius \u2014 even more extended than Jupiter\'s. ~15-20 Earth masses of heavy elements gradationally mixed into surrounding hydrogen. Temp ~21,000\u00b0F.'},{name:'Metallic hydrogen',pct:22,color:'#8090b0',label:'Metallic hydrogen (H\u2082)',desc:'Thinner than Jupiter\'s due to lower pressures. Generates Saturn\'s magnetic field (600\u00d7 Earth\'s, remarkably aligned with the rotation axis). Helium-rain region: helium separates from hydrogen and falls in droplets, releasing gravitational energy.'},{name:'Liquid molecular hydrogen',pct:15,color:'#a0b8d0',label:'Liquid hydrogen/helium (H\u2082/He)',desc:'Liquid H/He envelope where helium rain is most pronounced. Explains Saturn\'s excess heat output (2.5\u00d7 what it receives from the Sun).'},{name:'Atmosphere',pct:3,color:'#e0c880',label:'Hydrogen + helium (H\u2082 96% + He 3%)',desc:'Visible atmosphere with subtler bands than Jupiter. Less helium than Jupiter (it sank inward as helium rain). Winds up to 1,100 mph at equator. Hexagonal north-polar vortex is 20,000 miles across. Great White Storm eruptions every ~30 years.'}],note:'Mankovich & Fuller 2021 used kronoseismology \u2014 Saturn\'s f-mode oscillations imprinted on the C ring \u2014 to show a diffuse/fuzzy core reaching ~60% of radius, and a helium-rain region in the outer metallic-hydrogen layer. Helium depletion in the visible atmosphere matches. Saturn is less dense than water (0.687 g/cm\u00b3).'},uranus:{layers:[{name:'Rocky core',pct:20,color:'#808890',label:'Silicate + iron (Fe)',desc:'Small rocky/metallic core ~0.5-3 Earth masses, ~7,500 km diameter. Iron, nickel, and silicates. Temp ~9,000\u00b0F. Relatively cool compared to other giant planets \u2014 Uranus has almost no internal heat source (unique among gas/ice giants).'},{name:'Superionic ice layer',pct:30,color:'#3a88a0',label:'Superionic water ice (H\u2082O)',desc:'A solid oxygen lattice with freely mobile hydrogen protons \u2014 an exotic state of water ice first produced in the lab by Millot et al. 2019 (Nature) via laser-driven shock compression. Stable above ~50 GPa and ~2,000 K. Conducts electricity and likely generates Uranus\'s wildly tilted, offset magnetic field.'},{name:'Hot ice mantle',pct:25,color:'#60a0b0',label:'Liquid water/ammonia/methane (H\u2082O/NH\u2083/CH\u2084)',desc:'Hot dense fluid mixture of water, ammonia, and methane at 100+ GPa, above the superionic layer and below the gas envelope. Compositional gradients (Helled 2020, Vazan 2020) may make this a smooth transition rather than a sharp layer \u2014 Uranus may be non-convective below a certain depth, explaining its anomalously low heat flux.'},{name:'Atmosphere',pct:25,color:'#a0d8d8',label:'Hydrogen/helium/methane (H\u2082/He/CH\u2084 \u2014 83/15/2%)',desc:'~5,000 km H/He atmosphere with ~2.3% methane. Methane absorbs red wavelengths \u2192 blue-green color. Three haze/cloud layers: methane ice, hydrogen sulfide, ammonium hydrosulfide. Winds up to 560 mph. Extreme seasons from 98\u00b0 axial tilt.'}],note:'Uranus\'s magnetic field is offset by 1/3 of its radius and tilted 59\u00b0 from the rotation axis \u2014 best explained by a superionic water layer where the dynamo operates. Millot et al. 2019 (Nature) produced superionic ice in the lab via laser-driven shock compression. NASA\'s top-priority 2032+ flagship mission (Uranus Orbiter and Probe) will test this. Uranus is the coldest planet despite not being farthest from the Sun.'},neptune:{layers:[{name:'Rocky core',pct:22,color:'#707880',label:'Silicate + iron + nickel (Fe, Ni)',desc:'Rocky/metallic core ~1-2 Earth masses, ~8,000 km diameter. Iron, nickel, silicates. Temp ~12,000\u00b0F \u2014 much hotter than Uranus\'s core. This heat difference between nearly identical twins is a major unsolved mystery.'},{name:'Superionic ice layer',pct:28,color:'#2a6090',label:'Superionic water ice (H\u2082O)',desc:'Same exotic superionic water state as Uranus, but hotter. Solid oxygen lattice with mobile proton current \u2014 an effective dynamo source explaining Neptune\'s offset, tilted magnetic field (47\u00b0 from rotation axis).'},{name:'Hot ice mantle',pct:25,color:'#4080a0',label:'Liquid water/ammonia/methane (H\u2082O/NH\u2083/CH\u2084)',desc:'Hot dense fluid water, ammonia, and methane at 100+ GPa, above the superionic layer. Methane (CH\u2084) breaks apart at extreme pressure and carbon atoms are squeezed into diamond crystals \u2014 "diamond rain" \u2014 experimentally confirmed via laser-driven shock compression. This process may contribute to Neptune\'s excess heat.'},{name:'Atmosphere',pct:25,color:'#4488cc',label:'Hydrogen/helium/methane (H\u2082/He/CH\u2084 \u2014 80/19/1.5%)',desc:'~5,000 km atmosphere. Deeper blue than Uranus (possibly from an unknown chromophore). Most dynamic ice-giant atmosphere: 1,200+ mph supersonic winds, transient Great Dark Spots, bright methane cirrus clouds. Driven by internal heat, not solar energy.'}],note:'Neptune radiates 2.6\u00d7 more energy than it receives from the Sun, yet Uranus (nearly identical in size/composition) barely radiates any excess \u2014 one of planetary science\'s biggest mysteries. The similar offset, tilted magnetic fields of both planets point to a shared superionic-water dynamo mechanism.'},moon:{layers:[{name:'Solid inner core',pct:14,color:'#c0a080',label:'Iron (Fe)',desc:'Solid Fe inner core ~258 km radius (~14% of lunar radius). Confirmed as solid by Briaud et al. 2023 (Nature) using lunar laser ranging + GRAIL gravity. Temp ~2,400\u00b0F. Only ~1% of Moon\'s mass \u2014 the Moon is iron-depleted compared to Earth.'},{name:'Liquid outer core',pct:6,color:'#b09070',label:'Iron-sulfur (Fe-S)',desc:'Molten Fe-S outer core to ~362 km radius. Contains dissolved sulfur and carbon. Too small and cool today to sustain a dynamo \u2014 but the Moon DID have a magnetic field from ~4.2 to 3.6 Bya (recorded in Apollo rock samples, some stronger than Earth\'s today).'},{name:'Partial-melt layer',pct:8,color:'#8a7060',label:'Molten silicate rock',desc:'A ~150 km partially molten silicate layer at the core-mantle boundary, first inferred by Weber et al. 2011 from Apollo seismogram reanalysis and retained by subsequent models. Unique to the Moon \u2014 thermally insulates the core and explains persistence of the tiny liquid outer core today.'},{name:'Mantle',pct:69.7,color:'#a09080',label:'Silicate rock (olivine + pyroxene)',desc:'Single rigid silicate mantle, ~1,300 km thick. Magnesium-rich olivine and pyroxene. Source of mare basalt lavas that flooded impact basins from 3.9 to 3.1 Bya. Apollo seismic data shows the mantle is cold and rigid \u2014 moonquakes occur at ~700 km depth.'},{name:'Crust',pct:2.3,color:'#c0b8a8',label:'Anorthosite (feldspar-rich)',desc:'Feldspar-rich anorthosite \u2014 average ~43 km farside, ~34 km nearside (GRAIL 2012). Formed as light minerals floated to the top of a primordial magma ocean. Highland crust is ~4.4 billion years old.'}],note:'Mapped by Apollo seismometers (1969-1977), refined by GRAIL gravity (2012), and confirmed solid-inner-core by Briaud et al. 2023 (Nature) using LLR. The partial-melt layer above the core is the Moon\'s most distinctive interior feature. The ancient lunar magnetic field shut off as the tiny core cooled, but its record is preserved in Apollo basalt samples.'}};

