      type:'Rocky', maxMag:-1.9, angSizeRange:'5-13"',
      telescope:'Tiny disk shows phases like Venus. Requires very steady seeing near horizon. Best at greatest elongation. Surface featureless in amateur scopes.',
      facts:['Sunrise on Mercury is terrifying: the Sun appears 3× larger than on Earth and can briefly reverse direction in the sky due to orbital mechanics — it rises, stops, dips back below the horizon, then rises again','The temperature swing from day to night is the most extreme in the solar system: 800°F at noon to -290°F at midnight — a 1,100°F range on the same world','Mercury\'s iron core is so enormous (85% of radius) that the planet may be the stripped remnant of a much larger world — a giant impact blew away most of its rocky shell','Despite being closest to the Sun, Mercury has water ice in permanently shadowed polar craters where temperatures never rise above -280°F','The Caloris Basin impact was so violent it sent shockwaves through the entire planet, creating a "weird terrain" of jumbled hills on the exact opposite side of the globe','Mercury is shrinking — as its core slowly cools, the entire planet has contracted by ~4 miles in diameter, creating massive cliff-like scarps across its surface','A solar day on Mercury (sunrise to sunrise) lasts 176 Earth days — twice as long as its year. You\'d celebrate two birthdays before seeing a second sunrise','Mercury\'s surface looks almost identical to our Moon — covered in craters with no atmosphere to erode them. The Mariner 10 team initially thought they were looking at lunar photos'],
      missions:'MESSENGER (2011-15) mapped entire surface. BepiColombo (ESA/JAXA) arriving 2025 for polar orbit study.',
      tip:'Only visible low on horizon near sunrise/sunset. Best opportunities at greatest elongation (18-28° from Sun).'
