      diameter:30599, distSun:30.1, yearLen:'165yr', dayLen:'16h 6m', moons:16, gravity:'114% of Earth — noticeably heavier',
      type:'Ice giant', maxMag:+7.8, angSizeRange:'2.2-2.4"',
      telescope:'Requires telescope and finder chart. Appears as a tiny blue star-like dot — 2.3" disk barely resolvable. Triton (mag 13.5) visible in 10"+.',
      facts:['Neptune has the fastest winds in the solar system — over 1,200 mph (supersonic). For comparison, the strongest tornado ever recorded on Earth was 318 mph','Diamond rain is confirmed by lab experiments: methane breaks apart under extreme pressure and carbon atoms crystallize into actual diamonds that fall toward the core','Neptune is the only planet discovered by mathematics — its existence was predicted from wobbles in Uranus\'s orbit before anyone ever saw it through a telescope','The Great Dark Spot (discovered 1989) was an Earth-sized storm that simply vanished by 1994 — then new dark spots appeared elsewhere. Neptune\'s storms are born and die unpredictably','Neptune radiates 2.6× more heat than it receives from the Sun — the energy source is still debated. It may be gravitational contraction, exotic chemistry, or diamond rain releasing energy','Triton, Neptune\'s largest moon, orbits backwards and has nitrogen geysers shooting plumes 5 miles high — it\'s a captured Kuiper Belt dwarf planet slowly spiraling inward to its destruction','Neptune\'s blue color is deeper than Uranus\'s despite similar composition — an unknown chromophore compound absorbs red light more efficiently, and scientists still don\'t know what it is','Neptune\'s moon Triton is slowly spiraling inward — in a few billion years it will cross the Roche limit and be torn apart by tidal forces, likely forming a spectacular ring system rivaling Saturn\'s','Neptune\'s migration outward 4 billion years ago scattered Kuiper Belt objects everywhere and may have triggered the Late Heavy Bombardment that cratered the Moon and inner planets'],
      missions:'Only Voyager 2 (1989 flyby). No missions currently planned. Neptune/Triton Orbiter proposed but not funded.',
